{
 "id": "2026-latifzadeh-urban",
 "name": "Urban Green Space Affective Ratings",
 "description": "How does the amount of greenery in an urban scene shape how people feel about it? Participants see 30 photographs of streets, squares and green spaces taken by the authors at three sites in Esch-sur-Alzette / Belval, Luxembourg, one at a time in random order and self-paced, and rate each on four 7-point scales: emotional valence, safety, satisfaction and calmness. At the end they pick their three favourite places from a grid of all 30 and explain each choice. Ratings are keyed by the paper's image ID, so they join directly with the open dataset (27 participants, 810 trials, plus eye-tracking, pupil and EEG data; Zenodo record 18348490, CC BY 4.0), where images with more vegetation drew more attention and higher attractiveness ratings. Faithful replication: the authors' own 30 photographs. Rebuilt from Latifzadeh et al. (2026), CHI '26. Differences from the original study: (1) online, without eye tracking, pupillometry or EEG; (2) the full text was not reachable when the template was built, so the four questions and their end labels are written from the dataset's variable names (Emotional Valence, Safety, Satisfaction, Calmness, 1–7) rather than copied from the paper; check them against the paper before use; (3) photographs are resized to 1600 px.",
